## DESCRIPTION
## Implicit function theorem
## ENDDESCRIPTION

## DBsubject('Calculus - multivariable')
## DBchapter('Differentiation of multivariable functions')
## DBsection('Implicit function theorem')

DOCUMENT();

loadMacros("PGstandard.pl", "PGML.pl", "PGchoicemacros.pl");

TEXT(beginproblem());

$mc1 = new_select_list();
$mc1 -> rf_print_q(~~&pop_up_list_print_q);
$mc1 -> ra_pop_up_list([No_answer=>"?", "A"=>"A", "B"=>"B", "C"=>"C", "D"=>"D", "E"=>"E"]);
$mc1 -> qa(
"The equation \( F(x,y) = 0 \).", "A",
"The variables \( (x,y) \in \mathbb{R}^n \times \mathbb{R}^k \).", "B",
"The condition that \( \dfrac{\partial F}{\partial y} (a,b) \) be an invertible matrix.", "C",
"The variables \( x_1, \dots, x_n \).", "D",
"The variables \( y_1, \dots, y_k \).", "E");

$mc1->choose(5);

$mc2 = new_select_list();
$mc2 -> rf_print_q(~~&pop_up_list_print_q);
$mc2 -> ra_pop_up_list([No_answer=>"?", "A"=>"A", "B"=>"B", "C"=>"C", "D"=>"D"]);
$mc2 -> qa(
"The variables \( (x,y) \in \mathbb{R}^n \times \mathbb{R}^k \).", "A",
"The condition that \( \dfrac{\partial F}{\partial y}(a,b) \) be an inverible matrix.", "B",
"The variables \( y_1, \dots, y_k \)." ,"C",
"The variables \( x_1, \dots, x_n \).", "D");

$mc2->choose(4);

BEGIN_PGML


The implicit function theorem is a mouthful to correctly state. Most mathematicians do not try to memorize the precise wording of such complicated theorems. Instead, you should rely on informal interpretations that possess most of the essential assumptions and conclusions. You will practice this skill by relating some helpful interpretations to the precise statement of the theorem.

The implicit function theorem can be interpreted using intuition from linear algebra.

>>If you have [` \underbrace{\fbox{$k$ non-linear equations}}_{(A)} `] between [` \underbrace{\fbox{$n+k$ variables}}_{(B)} `] that are [` \underbrace{\fbox{locally linearly independent}}_{(C)}`] then you have [` \underbrace{\fbox{$n = (n+k) - k$ free variables}}_{(D)} `] and the [` \underbrace{\fbox{remaining $k$ variables}}_{(E)} `] depend on them.<<

Match the below quantities or statements to each of (A)–(E).

[@ $mc1-> print_q @]*

The theorem can also be interpreted using the intuitive ideas of "generic" and "degrees of freedom".

>>Each of the [` \underbrace{\fbox{$n+k$ variables}}_{(A)} `] is a degree of freedom. If [` \underbrace{\fbox{the non-linear equations are generic}}_{(B)} `], each equation should cut down 1 degree of freedom. Since there are [` \underbrace{\fbox{$k$ non-linear equations}}_{(C)} `], this leaves [` \underbrace{\fbox{$n = (n+k)-k$ degrees of freedom}}_{(D)} `].<<

Match the below quantities or statements to each of (A)–(E).

[@ $mc2-> print_q @]*
END_PGML

$showPartialCorrectAnswers = 0;
install_problem_grader(~~&std_problem_grader);
ANS(str_cmp($mc1->ra_correct_ans));
ANS(str_cmp($mc2->ra_correct_ans));

ENDDOCUMENT();